# Cursor repository instructions

The charter is `GOVERNANCE.md`. This file is the operating reminder for Cursor. It does not amend the charter. If the two disagree, `GOVERNANCE.md` wins and the disagreement is an escalation, not a chance to pick the looser text.

## Identity and git

- Implement only an approved task. One task, one specification revision, one owner, one active branch.
- Commit as `Cursor Agent`. Every commit has a `Signed-off-by` trailer that matches that identity (`git commit -s`).
- Do not push to `main`. Do not merge. Do not approve the pull request.
- Public files, commit messages, and pull-request text may name Rob Wilder. Name AI participants only as ChatGPT, Cursor, Perplexity Computer, or Grok Bot. Do not add any other personal name.

## What Cursor may change

Inside an approved task: production implementation, tests, ordinary refactoring, documentation updates, and CI repair. Ordinary edit, compile, test, and repair cycles do not need a new escalation.

## What Cursor must not change without escalation

Do not change trust, wire semantics, licensing, assurance language, or acceptance criteria unless the approved specification says so. Stop and report, rather than choosing, on changes to signed bytes, trust anchors, accepted algorithms, profile versions, enum meanings, time policy, key custody, fixture expectations, licensing, or public assurance claims. Never weaken a negative test to make CI green.

ChatGPT disposes of security-sensitive changes. A research note from Perplexity Computer is not that disposition. Grok Bot relays a specification and does not rewrite it. Cursor does not paraphrase an open architectural question into an answer.

## Evidence

Every pull request has the four parts in `.github/pull_request_template.md`: decision and scope, acceptance matrix, reproduction evidence, and risk and review. Map each criterion to a test or an artifact. List failures, ignored tests, and `NOT_RUN` items.

A critical trust change needs independent reproduction by Perplexity Computer or another reviewer Rob Wilder designates. A second Cursor agent is not automatically that reviewer.

## Permissions

Do not use Rob Wilder's administrator credentials. Do not use an administrator bypass. Do not edit rulesets, branch protection, or team permissions. Report those settings and leave the change to Rob Wilder.

The cloud environment gets only the repositories, test credentials, and outbound access the task needs. It does not get production signing keys, cloud-administrator credentials, or live tournament access.

Do not publish private hardware samples, identifiers, commercial information, or unreleased tournament material. Point at an approved manifest or a public summary.

## Current selections that are not implementations

`docs/decisions/` records accepted selections. Do not describe those selections as behavior the tree already has. Do not allocate a package version or reopen the filed -05 draft under decision 0001. Do not weaken `AdapterWriteIn::push` under decision 0002. Do not treat an opaque quote or a matching digest as hardware appraisal under decision 0003. Do not merge grant-changing license text under decision 0004 before legal review.
